From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mathieu GRENET <mathieu@mgth.fr>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 13:36:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 45/47] ad_iamf_iec61937: carry an IAMF track as IEC 61937
 bursts

`--ad=iamf-iec61937` decodes nothing: each temporal unit of an IAMF track
goes out as one burst of a private IEC 61937 data type (0x1D), on a
PCM-shaped carrier of 16-bit words at the track's rate, for an Omniphony
renderer at the other end of a link that carries PCM and so a clock
(Omniphony docs/iamf-transport-plan.md). The IEC assigned IAMF no data
type: no other equipment knows this one.

The burst period is the unit's duration in carrier frames, so the frames
keep their time and A/V sync works as with any output. The descriptors
from CodecPrivate lead the first burst and the first after a seek, as
they are, which opens a new sequence at the receiver; a copy with
obu_redundant_copy set leads one every --ad-iamf-iec61937-copy-interval
(1 s), for a receiver that joins late. The payload length goes on in Pc
bits 8-12 past the 65 535 bytes Pd counts. A unit longer than its period
leaves the period empty and says to widen the carrier
(--ad-iamf-iec61937-channels, 8 by default).

The frames are a compressed format, AF_FORMAT_S_IAMF, so no filter,
volume or resampler touches the words. af_fmt_is_spdif() tells the
compressed formats by their range rather than by a burst alignment,
which this one does not have.

- ao_pcm keeps the carrier's channel count instead of fitting it to a
  speaker layout.
- ao_pipewire presents the words as that many unpositioned S16 channels,
  with remixing, resampling, dither and restored volume off. It refuses
  the default sink and any fallback: on a loudspeaker the words are
  full-scale noise.

Checked against `orender wrap-iec61937`, which writes the same stream
from a standalone IAMF file: byte-identical through ao_pcm on Opus, FLAC
and object encodings at 2, 8, 16 and 24 channels. Through PipeWire into
a null sink and back from its monitor, 600 bursts of 600 came back
intact.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
